Yes, such extensions exist. Here is the proof.

**Construction.** Let $\omega = e^{2\pi i/3}$ be a primitive cube root of unity, and let $\alpha = \sqrt[3]{2}$ denote the real cube root of $2$. Set
$$E_1 = \mathbb{Q}(\alpha), \qquad E_2 = \mathbb{Q}(\omega\alpha).$$
Both are subfields of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \subseteq \mathbb{C}$, since $\alpha$ and $\omega\alpha$ are roots of $x^3 - 2 \in \mathbb{Q}[x]$, hence algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$.

**Both are finite extensions of $\mathbb{Q}$.** The polynomial $x^3 - 2$ is irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}$ by Eisenstein's criterion at the prime $2$. Both $\alpha$ and $\omega\alpha$ are roots of $x^3 - 2$, so $x^3 - 2$ is the minimal polynomial of each over $\mathbb{Q}$. Hence
$$[E_1 : \mathbb{Q}] = [E_2 : \mathbb{Q}] = 3,$$
so both are finite (degree $3$) algebraic extensions of $\mathbb{Q}$.

**They are isomorphic as fields.** Since $x^3 - 2$ is the minimal polynomial of both $\alpha$ and $\omega\alpha$, there is a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra isomorphism
$$\varphi : E_1 = \mathbb{Q}(\alpha) \longrightarrow E_2 = \mathbb{Q}(\omega\alpha)$$
determined by $\varphi(\alpha) = \omega\alpha$ (and $\varphi|_{\mathbb{Q}} = \mathrm{id}$). Concretely, every element of $E_1$ has the unique form $a + b\alpha + c\alpha^2$ with $a,b,c \in \mathbb{Q}$, and
$$\varphi(a + b\alpha + c\alpha^2) = a + b(\omega\alpha) + c(\omega\alpha)^2.$$
This is well defined and a field homomorphism because $\alpha$ and $\omega\alpha$ satisfy the same minimal polynomial; it is bijective since it is a nonzero homomorphism of fields (or by symmetry, since the inverse sends $\omega\alpha \mapsto \alpha$). Thus $E_1 \cong E_2$ as fields.

**They are distinct.** Since $\alpha = \sqrt[3]{2}$ is real, every element of $E_1 = \mathbb{Q}(\alpha)$ is real, i.e. $E_1 \subseteq \mathbb{R}$. On the other hand, $\omega\alpha = e^{2\pi i/3}\sqrt[3]{2}$ has nonzero imaginary part, so $\omega\alpha \notin \mathbb{R}$. If $E_2 \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, then $\omega\alpha \in E_2 \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, a contradiction. Hence $E_2 \not\subseteq \mathbb{R}$, and therefore $E_1 \neq E_2$.

**Conclusion.** $E_1 = \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt[3]{2})$ and $E_2 = \mathbb{Q}(\omega\sqrt[3]{2})$ are two distinct finite algebraic extensions of $\mathbb{Q}$, both contained in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and they are isomorphic as fields. $\blacksquare$

